  "journalText": "Beneath the argument lay a quieter disagreement. The fourth companion had marked the positions of the four companions upon the sanctuary floor. The warrior noticed that every path in the proposed escape led away from its center. He knelt and added a space where an answer could be given.\n\nThe warrior opposed the plan because he could not bear to lose his friend.\n\nHe also opposed a rescue that gave the people being saved no choice about its cost.\n\nThe new mark was not another route through the stone. It was room for a refusal. The fourth understood it, then continued with the plan. Neither had mistaken the other's concern for cowardice. Their disagreement survived because concern did not settle the question of authority.\n\nThe warrior's anger had two objects: the loss he feared, and the decision being made for them. Remembering only the first made him seem unable to understand the danger. Remembering both restores what he was defending. He could accept a risk without accepting another person's right to choose it for everyone.\n\nHe wanted his friend beside him. He wanted the others to be able to answer for themselves.",
  "wordCount": 192,
  "newlyRecoveredInformation": "Vanguard's objection included a concrete demand for consent, represented by a deliberately unfilled response space in the plan, rather than attachment alone.",
  "newlyAuthoredDetails": [
    "Positions marked on the floor and a space for refusal. This supplements, rather than changes, the recorded argument."
  ],
